p;quot;the Pure spirit allies are those who keep the conspiracy together&amp;quot;, there is still something to account for- that spirits are not nice. They can and will abuse people if and when they can. They want control. They want power. They want EVERYTHING- but the Mysteres don't give them everything. The Rites du Cheval, for whatever reason it may be, allow the spirits to ride their flesh and posses them while still leaving the hunter in control. That is not the norm- that's the exception, and that is important. Because if that's true, than perhaps the Mysteres are not as dupes as we might think they are.<br /> <br /> Let's start from the beginning. Like, as the very, very beginning.<br /> <br /> The Sundered World. <br /> <br /> It is easy to miss it, actually. It is merely a small section in the whole Vinca society as it was imagined in that Dark Era. There, among the many different roles which people may take upon themselves during those dark, dark times, we have a short mention of a role- but not a true role. It is generally a temporary act- serving as a priest for the old gods. In there, we can see what could easily be missed but definitely is the first instance of a Spirit Emissary- a person of any walks of life wearing the sacred mask and reaching out to the spirit world, bargaining with them on the benefit of their people. Some got Claimed, some got killed, but at its essence, what we have is everything that the conspiracy is all about- people who are willing to endanger their lives in order help their communities, being a bridge between the Flesh and Spirit. That role is so fundamental that it just makes sense to be there- yet for the Mysteres it is also something important to keep in mind. That those people have existed before the Forsaken and the Pure fell into a blood feud. Heck, before the world was even broken. When the spirits still roamed the world freely, they were there- and they were hunters. They had their own Vigil- putting their lives on the line in order to protect their communities against the horrors of the night, yet instead of killing those entities, they viewed them as gods which had to be appeased. Instead of using violence, they turned to bargain.<br /> <br /> And that, in its core, what this conspiracy is all about. Bargain with the Old Gods of the Shadow, in order to keep their people safe. And that, in my opinion, is very Vigilante.<br /> <br /> We have traces of this concept even in the modern organization- as it says, everyone can be a Mystere. No one is keeping the magic hidden- and yet, when the common person gets Ridden by the spirits, they don't become hunters. Heck, generally it does not end well for them at all. More than that, spirits are generally very, very jealous for their Ridden, as they are Essence generators. Considering how fractured the Mysteres are, there must be some unifying factor which keeps them together- again, those are the spirits, but in that end we should indeed ask ourselves how and why in Hell those spirits have decided to share their Ridden, and only possess them when there is an agreement between the sides. All of that makes me think that the Mysteres are not as dupes as it may be- because the very fact they have control, the fact that they are emissaries and not slaves and that their existence has been around for, well, eternity, yet only recently they can actually be defined as a conspiracy, all of that makes me think about what could have been the reason that the Mysteres are what they are, and not one of the many sad tragedies which spirit cults generally turn into.<br /> <br /> Also, did you noticed that once again we have monsters who through the Vigil maintain their humanity? Makes you see a recurring theme here, don't you think?<br /> <br /> Back to the topic- while the Mysteres are all about syncretism and many different versions for the truth and such, it is impossible to ignore the role of Voodoo in the formation of the group as a conspiracy. I mean, again, we always HAD spirit emissaries- but the Mysteres are something a bit different, due to the control they have over the possession. We can see it in the system of the Crossroads which they formalized in order to avoid spiritual manipulation. We can see it in the names of their Endowments. Heck, we can see it in their own name. But most importantly, we can see it in their history- for while the Mysteres have always existed, the origin of the group as a organization came from West Africa, where there was a community of local shamans which have established what looks like the first form of the conspiracy- one which got shattered during the trans Atlantic slave trade. And that is where things changed- because those shamans had to preserve their rites somehow in order to let them work. Centuries of traditions getting cut and broken and something needs to fill the missing parts, especially as those who took them from their lands have also tried to break their original belief system. And in order to keep it alive, in order to keep it going, in order to maintain who they were- they filled the pieces with whatever they found, with things which resembled their original faith, not the same yet pointing in the same direction. Voodoo was formed when native religions got folded into Christianity- and those shamans have used the same methods in order to complete their rites. Syncretism was the way to keep the magic going- and perhaps, it was also the way to free the spirit emissaries from the gods they were forced to serve.<br /> <br /> The core aspect of the Mysteres is that truth can only be understood through syncretism. The spirit world is so wide and strange that people can't just use one belief system in order to describe it- but every one get their own experience, get some things right, and by taking those parts which make sense and tying them together, something more true can form... freedom through the lack of definition. While the act of bargaining with the spirits was always an aspect of the spirit emissaries, the Mysteres changed things by embracing syncretism, having spirits which connect them all across the world by being willing to accept different faith because in the end, everything leads to the same truth. Voodoo was the basis for the Mysteres, and the fact that it is the most well known name for the conspiracy it definitely still is- it is very likely that Haiti and Brazil serve as the most unified part of the conspiracy, even though they membership includes other chapters all around the world. The fact here is that the Mysteres choose to be left undefined, and that their rites are all a part of a vast system which accepts everything and rejects nothing as long as it leads to truth, as long as it serves the Vigil, no matter the contradictions.<br /> <br /> And that is very much the antithesis for the spirit world.<br /> <br /> Spirits like to have things defined. They like things which have meaning. Resonance describes what makes sense for them to eat and what doesn't- and yet here you have a group that has some very close ties to the spirit world from one hand, yet embraces the multitude of definition. And I think that this is the key to understand the conspiracy- that by pure chance, they discovered the method where the shaman does not only bargain with the spirits for power, but keeps their own will during the possession. And that is because that by syncretism, they dilute the Resonance which ties them and the spirit together. In a way, just like the shamans of old, they are putting a mask in order to shield themselves from the spirits- yet this time, the mask traps the spirit. They define it according to their own system, and force to to be defined according to the will of the hunter, and that methodology makes sure that while the spirit will possess the hunter, it would not take control. Not because the spirit is nice, not because it wants to, because it can't. Because it has nothing to grasp upon. The Rites du Cheval are not as mixed to the point of a magath- but it is the same direction. The same direction as the Pure.<br /> <br /> Ha, you see what I did here? IT WAS ALL CONNECTED ALL ALONG<br /> <br /> While the spirits which serve the Pure are not magaths in general, the fact that both the Pure and their Firstborn are less defined and as such become more free may very well explain why the spirit emissaries are on their side instead of the Forsaken- not much about &amp;quot;free the spirits&amp;quot;, but something more metaphysical. In a way, the Mysteres are as &amp;quot;Pure&amp;quot; as humans may be, establishing a connection with Hisil based around not being well defined, and allowing the Shadow to fill the voids which miss in the formula. That passive control means that the Mysteres are not some lost souls in the grasp of the spirit world- but that they do know what they are doing. And even if the organization as a whole is unaware of why their rituals work, why anyone which hears the call of the spirits can cross the boundary between the worlds and remain unscathed, there is probably some understanding among those groups which act as the core of the organization (again, probably the Afro American chapter) for how syncretism serves as a protection mechanism, together with the system of bargains and masks. Anyone can be a Mysteres- if they can accept that the truth is wider than they can imagine, that all belief systems are valid and that by accepting all stories it is possible to patch out the true form of the world. And yeah, I am sure that the common person can truly accept that they are both right and wrong in the same time, and can totally accept when other people act in a way which they view as heresy. Because here is the thing- in order to be a Mysteres, you need to truly believe that you are right, and accept that others are as right as you are. You need to accept that Something Is There, and that you don't have all the answers. That there is a balance between truth and faith, and that faith leads to truth no matter the details. That antithesis for the spirit world is the way that the Mysteres can extract their will upon its denizens- in a way which could be more insidious than we can imagine.<br /> <br /> I mean, if we accept that through syncretism the Mysteres can maintain their control over the spirit world, and that spirits draw upon Resonance- what if the syncretic form of the Rites works as some form of a spiritual virus? An imprint of the hunter's will upon the spirit, formed as part of being Ridden and the bargain they make. And that syncretic imprint could modify the spirit a bit in order to make it fit into the system where all truths must be accepted and concepts must be diluted. And that spirit, now imprinted by the hunter, now leaves the bond at the end of the possession- but the imprint remains. And when a different hunter on the other side of the continent communes with the spirits in order to gain their help, they leave upon them the same imprint as the first spirit. And only spirit which carries the same imprint becomes a vessel through the syncretic network, allowing the imprints to commune with one another, transfer information and in a way, &amp;quot;possess&amp;quot; the spirit in order to make it into the form which the Mysteres call upon. That would unite the conspiracy's spirit allies as unaware carriers of a strange, almost magathic Essence which would allow two different entities across the world to momentarily become the same entity, transferring information across the globe for disparate communities all united through syncretism. A Purification of some sort. A magath of some sort. Not one spirit composed out of many different Resonances, but singles archetypes, the Loa, which are scattered among countless different pieces, countless different spirits, each of them being its own thing yet still part of a greater whole. Unity through syncretism.<br /> <br /> And that could serve as a good foundation for the conspiracy's endgame- if they knew it even existed.<br /> <br /> Here is the thing- we know what the Mysteres want: to unite all the worlds together. They want Flesh and Spirits to becomes one, to bring Pangaea back to life, yet they still want to protect their communities, which is why they don't surrender themselves to the spirits. That formulation of syncretic Essence across the globe may be the key for this- creating the greater Truth which would possess the Shadow, create a bridge between mortals and their gods by keeping humanity in control. A world where everyone is right, everyone can safely bargain with the spirits and everyone can coexist along one another. A world where the Flesh is bound to Spirit, yet the Shadow is much less defined, much more magath like, much more.. Pure. By binding Hisil through the syncretic network, by connecting into it the greater of gods and ties them to human will through bargains, it may be done- but the Mysteres are not aware of it, and as such they can't initiate it by themselves. Sure, some of the older and more unified chapters (again, Caribbean and South America) may have some concept of that potential, but that would require some decent coordination which may very well mean breaking some Mysteres cells from the network. However, it could be that the Mysteres don't actually need to do anything- the imprints and the Loa which rise from them probably start to unknowingly work towards that goal by themselves. They seek to spread, to manifest themselves through every spirit and any part of of the Shadow- and could very well be the ones to motivate the hunters in turn to keep going, pushing them to make more bargains, to propagate the Loa, ton unify more spirits through diversity. The Forsaken, of course, will be very much against such magath demigods- which could explain why the spirit allies so much motivate them to attack the Uratha. It is not about keeping the worlds apart. It is about stopping those spirits which seek to become a channel to the mysteries from taking over the Shadow. And the worse thing? That it can't actually be stopped. The Mysteres are everywhere, the Loa are already here. You'll have to kill every Mystere and every spirit which carries a Loa imprint when the hunters and the spirits are not even aware that they are a Mystere and a imprint. And even if you'll manage to find and purge them all, people would just return to speak with the spirits again later, and people would then start sharing their ideas and connect the dots and leave syncretic imprints over the Shadow- because that's the natural course of things.<br /> <br /> The Mysteres are inevitable. They just don't know it.<br /> <br /> Well, that's it for the Big Three! Next time we are moving to Mortal Remains. Hope you liked it!</div> </div> </div> </div>The mysteres are hated? I've always thought they were awesome lol, with an especially cool endowment. I guess I could understand if I thought of them as mere dupes, but i always thought the write up was very clear they weren't unthinking slaves to them (plus, considering the other conspiracies, I wasn't too surprised they'd honestly see the Pure as true good guys).<br /> <br /> Damn though. I really didn't realize they were hated. Like easily part of my top 5 most loved conspiracies<br /> <br /> Anyway, what would this pangaea look like though? It would certainly lead to a heiarchy much different from the original I assume","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-06T16:27:06-08:00","id":1474097,"title":"","content":"So sorta like the Keepers of the Covenant (Mage)? Hmm. Before Dark Eras gave us the partial update for the Keepers, my own take on them was that the Keepers\u2019 namesake \u2018covenant\u2019 was a promise of Pangaea\u2019s return (on the Awakened\u2019s terms, as usual) \u2014 mirroring this reimagining of of the Les Mysteres. Interesting.<br /> <br /> I admit, I\u2019m one of the dislikers (not strong enough to be hate though\u2026) of the as-presented LM, so this headcanon version of them is much more appealing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-07T04:00:25-08:00","id":1474117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473595-conspiracy-theory-the-supplementary?p=1474097#post1474097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So sorta like the Keepers of the Covenant (Mage)? Hmm. Before Dark Eras gave us the partial update for the Keepers, my own take on them was that the Keepers\u2019 namesake \u2018covenant\u2019 was a promise of Pangaea\u2019s return (on the Awakened\u2019s terms, as usual) \u2014 mirroring this reimagining of of the Les Mysteres. Interesting.<br /> <br /> I admit, I\u2019m one of the dislikers (not strong enough to be hate though\u2026) of the as-presented LM, so this headcanon version of them is much more appealing.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I haven't been thinking about the Keepers, that is indeed more or less the direction for the LM- even though that as regular humans, the stakes are much higher for them, which again, is what makes them hunters in my book.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I'm happy to hear you liked this take- honestly, the LM need to be revised considering how much Werewolf has changed between 1e and 2e. The conspiracy was very much grounded on the relation between the spirits and the Forsaken in 1e, and as those relations have shifted the conspiracy can no longer work as is, IMO. On the other hand, I have tried to stay true to the lore we already had- it was very tempting to make them more organized and connected, especially as I really think that while syncretism works for them their 1e version have went way too fart in decentralizing them and trying to encompass, well, EVERYTHING, but I still wanted to remain true to their basic themes, which meant keeping them somewhat passive and reacting to the world, with their Endowments granting them the needed freedom being more of an accident than a deliberate action. I also put a much greater emphasis on their role as spirit emissaries as being a version of a Vigil but that is something I think was already there- it is just that the original writeup has covered it under layers of Evil-Forsaken wordcount. I think that a 2e version of the LM should very much focus around those aspects, as it would give them a wider range and utilize the independence that the Rites du Cheval grant to their users.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-07T04:06:10-08:00","id":1474118,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Primordial newcomer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473595-conspiracy-theory-the-supplementary?p=1474096#post1474096\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Anyway, what would this pangaea look like though? It would certainly lead to a heiarchy much different from the original I assume</div> </div> </div> </div>As this writeup suggests, it would be less about hierarchy in general and more abut the Loa as bridges between humans and spirits. It would be a much more &quot;fair&quot; Shadow in terms of relations for humans, but probably not less wide and far more mad, with the Loa emanating out of spirits and connecting them to some kind of &quot;origin&quot; which no one is aware of. For the common werewolf, it would be as if the whole Hisil have turned into something between a magath and a idigam, with alien hive minds which forge the bonds between Flesh and Spirit, and Rank becoming tied to the transactions it has formed with humanity and how much the Loa is integrated into it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-07T16:11:02-08:00","id":1474141,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473595-conspiracy-theory-the-supplementary?p=1474118#post1474118\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> As this writeup suggests, it would be less about hierarchy in general and more abut the Loa as bridges between humans and spirits. It would be a much more &quot;fair&quot; Shadow in terms of relations for humans, but probably not less wide and far more mad, with the Loa emanating out of spirits and connecting them to some kind of &quot;origin&quot; which no one is aware of. For the common werewolf, it would be as if the whole Hisil have turned into something between a magath and a idigam, with alien hive minds which forge the bonds between Flesh and Spirit, and Rank becoming tied to the transactions it has formed with humanity and how much the Loa is integrated into it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Interesting. Also I respect that you did your best to frame this around their disorganization. I think i would struggle to stay true to an aspect of something I heavily dislike<br /> <br /> If I may ask,do you consider the Merrick institute too disorganized? Such a thing is actually a plot point with the 2 of the factions after all","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-08T03:57:59-08:00","id":1474183,"title":"","content":"Considering that Merrick are intentionally disorganized as they are literally the remnants of a conspiracy which got shattered by civil war, I won't say that they are too disorganized in their current state. If they are to remain a conspiracy in the long term and not getting disbanded they need to form some organization, eventually, especially as it is being composed mostly by children- it is either rebuild or die out, IMO","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-09T08:32:40-08:00","id":1474285,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Faithful of Shulpae</b></span><br /> <br /> Ha, yes, those guys.<br /> <br /> One of the things that I like to say about hunter that no organization is for everyone. We should remember that when the CofD was still the nWoD, Hunter took a somewhat &quot;out of the box&quot; method by not only giving you different Tiers of organizations to which your character could belong do, it also actively encouraged you to make new ones- something which was less supported in other gamelines where the number of Y splats was much more rigid both mechanically and conceptually. As such, Hunter gave a much wider navigation reach to explore new compacts and conspiracies, which is why we ended up having 40+ orgs in Hunter- which is way beyond any other Y splat from any other gameline (you could argue that it is because Hunter lacks a Z splat, but again, that's still out of the box compared to the rest of the gamelines during that time). As such, when you have so much variety of organizations, it is almost guaranteed that you would like some groups and dislike others, for variety of reasons- and for me, the Faithful of Shulpae are found in the &quot;dislike&quot; pile.<br /> <br /> Now, I am not saying that they are not appropriate to the setting or that they are not hunters- but I have some... issues with their Endowment. I mean, yeah, I am a huge fan of the Abbey doing horrible things to monsters because &quot;monsters don't count&quot;, but actually eating other people, even if they are monstrous, just doesn't sit well in me. However, I do think that they make sense within the setting, and use themes which are appropriate to their main antagonist- which is more than the original reception that they had among some of the fans, especially Mummy fans.<br /> <br /> Now, this is a thing that Hunter gets ALL THE TIME, especially during the early days of the game, and which it still gets from some fans of other gamelines- very much like the &quot;anti magic lobotomy&quot; thing that Mage hunters hate (nevermind that the concept actually came from Mage but that's another topic), every time that Hunter goes out of the box and allows Hunters to do things which are not &quot;allowed&quot; by other gamelines, some fans go into rage over it- and that was true even about the Faithful of Shulpae. Why, you ask? Because the Faithful eats mummies, in for some reason, Mummy 1e have took the wordcount to mention that the flesh of a mummy is inedible for regular beings. While for me it looked like a weird argument considering that the Faithful use magic as part of their Endowments and for me magic solves everything, but some people had a issue with it. Heck, not just &quot;some people&quot;- iirc, one of the writers (or maybe even the devs) has pronounced dislike for the idea, claiming that Mummy's team wasn't consulted with and said &quot;the Mummies they eat are not <i>our</i> Mummies&quot;. Which, IMO, is really an overreaction.<br /> <br /> And we should actually be thankful for that overreaction.<br /> <br /> Now, when Hunter does something which another gameline does not accept, there are two methods- either to deny it and go against it with all of your available wordcount (like how Bleach's mangake did his best to drive the anime team into the corner in terms of canon) or try to find a way to integrate it into the wider canon (like almost any other sane mangake does). For reasons we are not going to discuss, Mage took mostly the first path- while Mummy took the second one, for while the original reaction was denial, sometime afterwards we got Sothis Ascends- and in there, we had the Cannibal Hymn, dedicated for King Unas, who was blessed to eat the gods themselves.<br /> <br /> Really, it can be more clear than that, right?<br /> <br /> The history of the Faithful may be somewhat shallow and full of holes, but their origin seems to be very clear- that during the reign of King Unas, his Shuankhsen advisor have manipulated his desire for immortal lives and experimented on him, giving him to eat the flesh of the mummies while using all kinds of strange occult methods, making him less of a human and more like a Lifeless. That should not come as a surprise to anyone- Endowments which allow someone to eat a mummy are, like 95% to come from the Devourer in some way, shape or form, with the last 5% being dedicated for the Prince of 1000 Leaves and the Eater of Names. Now, the interesting thing is that this experiment have worked- King Unas left his own tomb, which says that through the use of the forbidden rituals he have became somewhat of a pseudo-Shuankhsen- which, again, fits the recurring theme of monsters becoming hunters through the use of Endowments.<br /> <br /> It should be stressed, however, that Unas was not, by modern standards, a FoS hunter- for the simple reason he was still not a hunter. He was very much a monster, driven by hunger and unstable powers. Probably he had more in common with a Deviant than with a hunter. However, we do know that by the time of Hawara the followers of King Unas have establish a certain form of a organization- the Maw of Unas, yet instead of using Sekhem Sorcery they have turned to utilizing alchemy instead, targeting Prometheans in order to fulfill their hunger. That is an important part of their history, because it 1) gives them a form of shared rituals and organization and b) opens the ability of devouring monsters into more than just mummies. Sure, at this point at time we are yet to talk about hunters, but we do have a precedence of alchemists becoming hunters in the form of the AO. So taking it all into account, we do have an instance where Sorcery and Alchemy get melded together, united by raw and primordial hunger- something which probably facilitated the formation of the modern Faithful.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, this is the last piece of information we are getting about the Faithful- unless they'll show up in one of the in line products for Hunter or Mummy. What we do know that sometimes after that point, the alchemists and sorcerers of the Maw would have become more and more specialized in self transformation- the use of Sekhem Sorcery in order to draw upon the powers of the Devourer and the use of alchemy in order to transform their own bodies into being capable to not just digest monstrous power, but also to absorb and manifest it within themselves. The fact that they currently name themselves after Shulpae leads us to believe that for whatever reason it may be, the cult have moved its center of operation from Egypt to Mesopotamia, as Shulpae is a lesser known god of the Feast. He also seems to have aspects of warrior god and ruler of the animal kingdom- as well as some connection to Jupiter. Now, this last part is <i>important</i>, because in Babylonian astrology, Jupiter was also connected to Marduk- which, in turn, is connected to the Nibiru and the Vigil as a whole. Does it mean that the FoS are the Nibiru? I doubt it- but it may be a sign that this point in time was when the Faithful actually became hunters- their foul rituals have reached perfection, creating their twisted religion where the gods are to be eaten as worship. This is interesting because we in a way, the Faithful are the least &quot;hunters&quot; among the different hunter groups- simply because they do not, in their POV, hunt. They believe that they worship monsters by eating them, that they are doing sacred acts of sharing divinity. By all accounts, they should be a mystery cult- but the fact that they are still hunters, which means that they follow the Code and maintain the Vigil, means that they still believe in the dichotomy between monsters and people, and that people should hunt the darkness. It is just that their method of &quot;hunt&quot; is corrupted by the rite of Anthropophagy- which correlates to another concept which I have noticed in hunter. The theme of sins.<br /> <br /> Now, this is tangent to the subject but should be still talked about in this context- certain Hunter organizations directly draw power from monsters, and what they have in common is that their methods could very well correspond to some of the Seven Deadly Sins. Is it a planned theme? I doubt it, but it is still there. The FoS are all about Gluttony, for example, while the Promethean Brotherhood have been very much Envy like. Add Greed to the Hototogisu, Pride to Cheiron and Sloth to the AKD (they use relics instead of actively taking powers from monsters), and we can see the pattern emerging to a degree (you can argue about the details, sure, but a pattern is a pattern). Why it is important? No idea- but the Seven Deadly Sins are cool- and they resonate strongly with what the hunters are doing. Those hunters, basically do the greatest sin against the Vigil- becoming monsters. That may mean that each such &quot;path for monstrosity&quot; is expressed through a Sin derived from that Original Sin- which gives a lot of room for hunters who are NOT good people and which could be viewed very much as NOT hunters- they sin against humanity, but because that those sins still serve the Vigil they are still hunters. A liminal place between hunters and monsters, so to speak. I assume that if the Abbey were to become a conspiracy it would take the spot for Lust and Skin and Bones' Dies Irae are all about Wrath- but again, this is a whole different theory for a different time.<br /> <br /> This all discussion is here in order to justify their position as hunters- because apparently the Sins of the Vigil are an important part of it, which could lead to a meta conspiracy theory. And once we establish their place within the Vigilante community, we can talk about another, stranger theme for the FoS- that people don't join the conspiracy. They are chosen. Some people who are exposed to a mummy just discover the hunger and the ability to devour the gods within themselves. It is not some taught rite- it is a ability which manifest itself within some people. This is why the FoS accept anyone who stumbles upon the Feast and wants to join- they are guided there by Fate. And that is important because as we know, Fate plays a big role in Mummy. Now, if Anthropophagy is not a learned skill but something some people just have and is being awakened when they are exposed to a Deathless, somewhat like a twisted form of a Sybaris, but we do know that it once was- then what has happened? How did the ceremonies and rituals of the Cannibal Hymn have became embedded into humanity? Was it a flux- or some intentional plan?<br /> <br /> It could very well that it was just Fated to be.<br /> <br /> Now, the thing we need to remember is that while Open Rites could be used by anyone, in general it is still something you need to learn. More than that, as it seems, not everyone can become a Faithful- or else, all Arisen would have been devoured already. Again, the process of becoming a Faithful does resonate well with a reaction for a Sybaris- yet where people usually are driven mad with fear, they feel desire, hunger, need to consume. That means that some people just have some potential to become mummy eaters- they just need initial exposure, and Fate would lead them towards the conspiracy, which means that Fate has plans for the Faithful. Now, let's take a step back- we already know that their Endowment is the result of both Sorcery and Alchemy- and it could very well be that the acts of alchemy have transformed the very essence of those who practiced the rites- allowing them to pass them on with their blood. Yet unlike other conspiracies, the Faithful do not keep track after those bloodlines- some chapters may, but the conspiracy as a whole doesn't worry about it. Why? Because Fate is there to make sure that the conspiracy will be united. It could very well, be that the FoS were Fated to be, an answer of humanity to the horror of Irem and a challenge to the Judges. People who are outside the Law of Suffering. In a way, it is not the Faithful who are Vigilante- it is Fate. It is the will of humanity who rebels against the harsh judgement of Duat and claims the precious Sekhem of the Deathless for themselves. Sure, the Devourer is there, but she may be just as exploited by the Endowment as the Judges- her essence is what allows the hunters to devour, yet the Sekhem is taken by humanity instead of going down her gulp. Humanity decided to eat its own gods, a primal answer forged by rituals imbued into the fabric of chosen people, the lineage of Unas and his Maw. Heck, Unas himself may very well be Shulpae. Heck, he may even still be around.<br /> <br /> So that's it? The only thing which holds this conspiracy together is Fate and the will of humanity? Are we having yet another decentralized conspiracy kept alive by supernatural forces?<br /> <br /> Not exactly. After all, we still have the temple guardians.<br /> <br /> The Keepers are basically the foundation of the conspiracy- while everyone who feels the pull to the Feast can join, the Keepers are those who maintain the feast, tend the sleeping gods and keep the whole structure alive. Those hunters are very, very old- as one of the powers they are the most likely to eat is that of immortality. They are ancient and powerful and could, potentially, transcend the usual limitations of the Endowment, allowing them to reach a Mummy like state in terms of power- something very much like Unas. We don't know how many such &quot;ascended&quot; devourers&quot; we have among the hunters, but considering their immortality you don't need a lot in order to keep this whole thing going- just enough to manage the conspiracy throughout the ages, especially considering that Fate is on their side. Maybe King Unas is part of those ancients, still roaming the world centuries after his supposed death and growing fat with power. Maybe he is long gone, just a memory by the rest of the immortals which rule the conspiracy. Whatever the case may be, the thing is that while the FoS may seem decentralized, there is a strong core which keeps them going, coordinating them and probably have long term plans for the world.<br /> <br /> What kind of plans, you ask?<br /> <br /> Eating the gods, of course.<br /> <br /> If we are to say that the FoS are humanity's response to the Rite of Return, a demand from mankind's side to return the Sekhem which was stolen from them, then it is only natural that the next step is not simply eating the gods during their periodical awakenings- it is to eat the Rite itself, devour the cycle and feast upon the divine. The Great Feast, so to speak- eating the Beasts of Sea, Sky and Earth, eating the powers who decry law and order... heck, maybe eating the Devourer herself. Human gluttony knows no bound after all. They want to eat the gods- the Nameless Gods, the Judges, Azer, Sutekh- everything. They want to ascend and take their place, to devour Duat as a whole. Something like what the Shan'iatu have tried, but from the human side. Heck, they may want to devour the Stars of A'aru themselves, becoming eternal and forever denying entropy, forever damning the Devourer into eternal hunger for they have eaten everything and didn't left her even a scrap. And here is the thing- the FoS are <i>not</i> selfish about their paradise. They don't exclude people from the Feast- in fact, they probably want everyone to join. They want everyone to take part in this act of holy worship. In this world, everyone can become gods through the act of eating those they worship- there are no sins, not virtues. Everyone are accepted in open arms, to bring triumph over those who viewed humanity as nothing but a sacrifice. The Devourer may have accidently just created her own worse enemy- something hungrier than herself.<br /> <br /> King Unas wanted to eat the gods. Time to join his feast.<br /> <br /> (Next time- the Knights of St Adrian! Stay tuned)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","user":"LostLight","date":". 02-25-2022, 06:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.