(they use relics instead of actively taking powers from monsters), and we can see the pattern emerging to a degree (you can argue about the details, sure, but a pattern is a pattern). Why it is important? No idea- but the Seven Deadly Sins are cool- and they resonate strongly with what the hunters are doing. Those hunters, basically do the greatest sin against the Vigil- becoming monsters. That may mean that each such &amp;quot;path for monstrosity&amp;quot; is expressed through a Sin derived from that Original Sin- which gives a lot of room for hunters who are NOT good people and which could be viewed very much as NOT hunters- they sin against humanity, but because that those sins still serve the Vigil they are still hunters. A liminal place between hunters and monsters, so to speak. I assume that if the Abbey were to become a conspiracy it would take the spot for Lust and Skin and Bones' Dies Irae are all about Wrath- but again, this is a whole different theory for a different time.<br /> <br /> This all discussion is here in order to justify their position as hunters- because apparently the Sins of the Vigil are an important part of it, which could lead to a meta conspiracy theory. And once we establish their place within the Vigilante community, we can talk about another, stranger theme for the FoS- that people don't join the conspiracy. They are chosen. Some people who are exposed to a mummy just discover the hunger and the ability to devour the gods within themselves. It is not some taught rite- it is a ability which manifest itself within some people. This is why the FoS accept anyone who stumbles upon the Feast and wants to join- they are guided there by Fate. And that is important because as we know, Fate plays a big role in Mummy. Now, if Anthropophagy is not a learned skill but something some people just have and is being awakened when they are exposed to a Deathless, somewhat like a twisted form of a Sybaris, but we do know that it once was- then what has happened? How did the ceremonies and rituals of the Cannibal Hymn have became embedded into humanity? Was it a flux- or some intentional plan?<br /> <br /> It could very well that it was just Fated to be.<br /> <br /> Now, the thing we need to remember is that while Open Rites could be used by anyone, in general it is still something you need to learn. More than that, as it seems, not everyone can become a Faithful- or else, all Arisen would have been devoured already. Again, the process of becoming a Faithful does resonate well with a reaction for a Sybaris- yet where people usually are driven mad with fear, they feel desire, hunger, need to consume. That means that some people just have some potential to become mummy eaters- they just need initial exposure, and Fate would lead them towards the conspiracy, which means that Fate has plans for the Faithful. Now, let's take a step back- we already know that their Endowment is the result of both Sorcery and Alchemy- and it could very well be that the acts of alchemy have transformed the very essence of those who practiced the rites- allowing them to pass them on with their blood. Yet unlike other conspiracies, the Faithful do not keep track after those bloodlines- some chapters may, but the conspiracy as a whole doesn't worry about it. Why? Because Fate is there to make sure that the conspiracy will be united. It could very well, be that the FoS were Fated to be, an answer of humanity to the horror of Irem and a challenge to the Judges. People who are outside the Law of Suffering. In a way, it is not the Faithful who are Vigilante- it is Fate. It is the will of humanity who rebels against the harsh judgement of Duat and claims the precious Sekhem of the Deathless for themselves. Sure, the Devourer is there, but she may be just as exploited by the Endowment as the Judges- her essence is what allows the hunters to devour, yet the Sekhem is taken by humanity instead of going down her gulp. Humanity decided to eat its own gods, a primal answer forged by rituals imbued into the fabric of chosen people, the lineage of Unas and his Maw. Heck, Unas himself may very well be Shulpae. Heck, he may even still be around.<br /> <br /> So that's it? The only thing which holds this conspiracy together is Fate and the will of humanity? Are we having yet another decentralized conspiracy kept alive by supernatural forces?<br /> <br /> Not exactly. After all, we still have the temple guardians.<br /> <br /> The Keepers are basically the foundation of the conspiracy- while everyone who feels the pull to the Feast can join, the Keepers are those who maintain the feast, tend the sleeping gods and keep the whole structure alive. Those hunters are very, very old- as one of the powers they are the most likely to eat is that of immortality. They are ancient and powerful and could, potentially, transcend the usual limitations of the Endowment, allowing them to reach a Mummy like state in terms of power- something very much like Unas. We don't know how many such &amp;quot;ascended&amp;quot; devourers&amp;quot; we have among the hunters, but considering their immortality you don't need a lot in order to keep this whole thing going- just enough to manage the conspiracy throughout the ages, especially considering that Fate is on their side. Maybe King Unas is part of those ancients, still roaming the world centuries after his supposed death and growing fat with power. Maybe he is long gone, just a memory by the rest of the immortals which rule the conspiracy. Whatever the case may be, the thing is that while the FoS may seem decentralized, there is a strong core which keeps them going, coordinating them and probably have long term plans for the world.<br /> <br /> What kind of plans, you ask?<br /> <br /> Eating the gods, of course.<br /> <br /> If we are to say that the FoS are humanity's response to the Rite of Return, a demand from mankind's side to return the Sekhem which was stolen from them, then it is only natural that the next step is not simply eating the gods during their periodical awakenings- it is to eat the Rite itself, devour the cycle and feast upon the divine. The Great Feast, so to speak- eating the Beasts of Sea, Sky and Earth, eating the powers who decry law and order... heck, maybe eating the Devourer herself. Human gluttony knows no bound after all. They want to eat the gods- the Nameless Gods, the Judges, Azer, Sutekh- everything. They want to ascend and take their place, to devour Duat as a whole. Something like what the Shan'iatu have tried, but from the human side. Heck, they may want to devour the Stars of A'aru themselves, becoming eternal and forever denying entropy, forever damning the Devourer into eternal hunger for they have eaten everything and didn't left her even a scrap. And here is the thing- the FoS are <i>not</i> selfish about their paradise. They don't exclude people from the Feast- in fact, they probably want everyone to join. They want everyone to take part in this act of holy worship. In this world, everyone can become gods through the act of eating those they worship- there are no sins, not virtues. Everyone are accepted in open arms, to bring triumph over those who viewed humanity as nothing but a sacrifice. The Devourer may have accidently just created her own worse enemy- something hungrier than herself.<br /> <br /> King Unas wanted to eat the gods. Time to join his feast.<br /> <br /> (Next time- the Knights of St Adrian! Stay tuned)</div> </div> </div> </div>Also important to note that the Keepers know how to keep their gods in slumber, which means very bad things for a mummy (or any monster who enters a deep sleep) whose cult has been subverted without his knowing.<br /> <br /> Also damn, it seems I like the hated factions lol (though I can understand hating the les mysteres a lot more than these guys). At least I know the Knights of Saint Adrian are well liked.<br /> <br /> With that out of the way, I do think the FoS are an excellent question to &quot;just how twisted can hunters be while still being hunters&quot;. It's an interesting thing, as Hunters ARE different from the more &quot;generic&quot; cultists. It really makes you wonder if the Vigil is merely pseudo mystical, or if there is a greater power behind it, perhaps in the astral or supernal (or both). I tend to be in the mindset that as a person sees humanity as something not to be mere slaves to monsters, that qualifies themselves as hunters. After all, the abbey do all kinds of things with monsters including being fed upon, but while still humanity as a sacred state to be","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-09T10:40:34-08:00","id":1474301,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Primordial newcomer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473595-conspiracy-theory-the-supplementary?p=1474296#post1474296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Also damn, it seems I like the hated factions lol (though I can understand hating the les mysteres a lot more than these guys). At least I know the Knights of Saint Adrian are well liked.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just to clarify- I don't think that the FoS are widely disliked. I personally don't like them, and the Mummy fandom had issues with them when they were first published due to the reasons detailed in here. After their integration through Sothis Ascends that objection became obsolete and I think that while they may not be widely liked they are also not being actively disliked. Like, people can say that they like them without getting a backlash from the fanbase, and you won't hear people ranting about them now like they sometimes do about Les Mysteres or nuVASCU","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-09T14:40:21-08:00","id":1474327,"title":"","content":"Fan of the Feast here, nothing else to contribute so far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-09T15:01:02-08:00","id":1474329,"title":"","content":"Saying the truth, I think that it is because the FoS are probably the most honest conspiracy in the setting- they are very sincere about what they are, what do they want and how do they get it. They don't really hide things from the members of the conspiracy and just accept anyone who shows potential and will to join the Feast. Oh, sure, I could have tried to pull something more insidious here- but really, the worst I could do is say &quot;hey, those guys unknowingly serve the Devourer&quot; and people would say &quot;Did you know that the skies are blue?&quot;. The fact that Unas may still be alive and that there may be other ancient immortals in the conspiracy is, like, the most I could squeeze out of the lore we have, and so most of my analysis went on the origin of their Endowment. Even their endgame is not a twist as much as it is a natural progression: they want to eat the gods, they discover that those gods has bigger gods, they want to eat the bigger gods. Overall, makes sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-09T18:39:02-08:00","id":1474342,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473595-conspiracy-theory-the-supplementary?p=1474285#post1474285\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because the Faithful eats mummies, in for some reason, Mummy 1e have took the wordcount to mention that the flesh of a mummy is inedible for regular beings.</div> </div> </div> </div>It had a pretty clear basic reason for doing that, actually, which I observed in a Beast thread, of all places:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MtC1e Core</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Unclean Remains: Natural animals can subconsciously sense the traces of undeath within a mummy\u2019s corpse and will never disturb or eat such material, even if starving. Even if a creature was forced to consume a mummy\u2019s remains, the material would not digest and would be vomited up or otherwise expelled in short order.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/765301-begotten-and-wicked-children-of-the-dark-mother?p=768613#post768613\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">[T]he Faithful's rules reference the amount of edible flesh on a target supernatural (which doesn't have to be &quot;dead&quot; at the time of consumption and in fact is mechanically incentivized to be as intact as possible), the sahu is a simulation of flesh made from memory and congealed necromancy, and <u>Unclean Remains is pretty clearly an insurance quality against &quot;jackals ate your guildmaster's body when nobody was looking&quot; rather than a defensive measure against cannibalism for a splat whose principle antagonists specifically include cannibal mummies.</u></div> </div> </div> </div>It was there to ensure that nobody needed to ask why a splat that exists as mummified corpses ninety-nine percent of the time hasn't been gradually consumed by rats every time one of them loses a cult and goes back to sleep before they can pick up the pieces. This is less of a concern in 2e between the timeless nature of the Arisen, the expanded ability to reincarnate into a new body, and the universalizing of an existing cultivatable quality allowing you to spontaneously generate a cult to serve you in time for your next awakening even if all your cultists are killed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T13:00:46-08:00","id":1474511,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b>Knights of St Adrian</b></span><br /> <br /> Seemingly one of the most modern and relatively localized conspiracies, the KoSA are basically the mercenaries of God. Composed of skip tracers and bail bondsmen, of truck drivers and bikers, the KoSA are so young that their founder is still around, and their reach and methods seem very, well, American. While they are very, very cool, their culture and aesthetics makes it hard for me to view them operating much with the same methods outside of the USA, as skip tracing as a practice is not something I was even aware of before reading about the KoSA, and I viewed as weird until I actually heard a interview of a bail bondsman on YouTube which explained how it actually works. Now, I don't know how common it is around the world- I only know that where I live it isn't really a thing, and as such it makes the KoSA something very, let's say, &quot;exotic&quot;- nevermind how appropriate they are in the game, especially in the context of Demon.<br /> <br /> When the information about the conspiracy was just released, some people were a bit confused about what role do truck drivers and bikers have in a espionage game. The answer was, of course, subversion- the KoSA are the manifestation of brute force. They don't just track down demons, they are also able to punch them so hard in the face that they get out of Cover, allowing angels to track them down. Now, while you could assume that people would get all &quot;You Can't Do It Hunter&quot; over it like they sometimes do, thankfully this conspiracy was written together with Demon's team, which gave a free pass for every crazy thing that the KoSA can do that violates some parts of Demon. Overall, I think that this conspiracy was very much well received, between being a thematic opposition to demons, serving as the Hounds of God, and overall being just plain cool as stigmatic Hell's Angels who go around and kick ass.<br /> <br /> Again, notice that the KoSA keep the theme of monstrous power turned into Endowment- while Stigmatic are generally not being viewed as monsters (having Supernatural Merits instead of Dread Powers), gaining Stigmata could very well count as &quot;power from a monstrous source&quot;- something which the KoSA take upon themselves and utilize in a very distinct way to hunt down demons. Now, something that should be noted is that Stigmata is something which is generally viewed as &quot;accident&quot;- someone stumbles upon a Infrastructure, gets scarred by it and develop strange powers as a result. The more reliable way that hunters of the KoSA gain those abilities makes me wonder if perhaps a more intrinsic system is at play- something which is very God Machine appropriate, as it acts as systems within systems. Something similar to adoption which makes the KoSA &quot;demon blooded&quot; of some kind? Perhaps, but whatever the case may be this conspiracy indeed plays well with the continued theme of &quot;monsters into people through the Vigil&quot; which many conspiracies seem to share.<br /> <br /> Another thing to note is that while I don't know if it was intended, the KoSA very much reminds me of the movie Frailty, where <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> you have a family chosen by a angel to kill out &quot;demons in human skin&quot;, with those people being able to see through the &quot;human guise&quot; by touch (with demons in that context being just horrible human beings). Not the same thing as the KoSA exactly, but still have enough in common for me to assume it served as an inspiration </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Anyway, overall it seems like there isn't much to chew on in relation to KoSA- we know they were founded by Casey Howards during the 90's, making the conspiracy around 40 years old at best. We know that they have the God Machine as their client, sending them to hunt demons through their contact with their angelic &quot;client&quot;. Really, while the conspiracy is very clearly used by the God Machine, it is hard to say that the KoSA are manipulated by it- they know what they are up to and what they are doing. For them, it's a combination of it being a business and how demons take away parts of people lives in exchange for &quot;benefits&quot;, while God simply rewards them for their loyalty and use. There is not mystery, nothing is hidden- the contract between God and His Hounds is very clear and well understood. Nothing weird, nothing out of the ordinary. Just common people killing demons, move along, move along.<br /> <br /> At least, that's what you could think from the writeup alone.<br /> <br /> Here is the thing- the fact that the KoSA exist makes sense. God needs his soldiers, after all. However, the fact that they are so young is... weird. Sure, with the support of God 30-40 years is nothing in order to make a certain group into a global power- but why did the God Machine waited for so long to create such a organization? I mean, if It can bless humans with demon hunting powers, why it doesn't, like, grant them to any cult in the world? Or at least, any cult which shows loyalty to it? Many, many organizations would have shown great interest in having a regular connection to the God Machine which would feed them info about the location of demons and give them the powers to hunt them down. Why the KoSA and not anyone else? Why to take so long? Why to focus its attention on the US and ignore the rest of the world?<br /> <br /> Thankfully, those questions get answered, at least partially, in Dark Eras 2.<br /> <br /> In Empire of Dust and Gold we get a sidebar which details a organization which is uncannily similar to the modern KoSA- the Soldiers of Jabreel. Again, we have a organization of militaristic hunters who serve as &quot;holy hunters&quot; chosen by God to fight demons, including special abilities bestowed upon them which allow those hunters to fight their enemies heads on. Now. while the writeup does not outright connects them to the modern conspiracy, the writer have confirmed that the Soldiers were a different incarnation of the Knights, one which was more appropriate for the spirit of the Era and which fit better the local area.<br /> <br /> One which may be still fitting in modern times.<br /> <br /> Here is the thing- technically we are not aware of the SoJ getting disbanded at any point. Like, we can assume it happened (especially after, you know, colonialism), but we don't have an actual date for when did the Soldiers expired and the Knights were recreated. As far as we are aware, the Soldiers could still be there, maintaining a Vigil all across the Middle East or even the Muslim World in general. If that's not the case, however, it could very well be that the KoSA were founded after the SoJ got the final nail in their coffin, with the angel leaving the old conspiracy's remnants behind and starting a new project in the territory of the current global superpower. We also don't know if there were other incarnations between the SoJ and the KoA, which potentially means that we have the same angel moving from one local chapter to another, making a conspiracy and moving along when their time comes to an end as the demons manage to finally destroy the conspiracy. That could make some sense- especially as if it is the same angel making those different incarnations, it could be that this specific angel was granted the ability to gift mortal with divine power as a unique feature, being a part of its function. On the other hand, the idea of an angel operating for a millennia so close to humans and <i>not Falling</i> should be terrifying for an Unchained- as it means that this angel is not simply powerful, but is also very, very loyal. Archangel level of loyalty, perhaps. On the other hand, if the SoJ are not defuncted, it could be that this angels operates a number of different chapters of the same conspiracy all across the world, either having multiple Covers in the same time or, at the very least, maintaining a network of angels which operates those different mechanisms in their quest for hunting demons. If that's the case, why did this angel waited for so long before creating a new chapter in the US? Unknown, but it could be related to certain conditions unrelated to the KoSA- like requiring different Infrastructures being formed and maintained in order to produce the stigmatic abilities of the group. Maybe the God Machine wanted to maintain a strong grasp in the US before establishing a demon hunting group, for example, or it could be something more occult. Who knows? But what we get is that there is a strong possibility that the KoSA are not the only form of the conspiracy, and that either it is just the latest in a successive line of incarnations or it is the current most powerful chapter of the same, worldwide organization.<br /> <br /> Now that we have both the SoJ and the KoSA, we can make some comparison between those two orgs, and from those we can get some very clear parallels- sure, there is the whole &quot;divine soldiers of God&quot;, but that's obvious. What is less obvious is the connection between their &quot;patrons&quot;- Saint Adrian is one of the most popular Military Saints, and Jabreel is the Arabized version of Gabriel- again, an angel named &quot;God's Might&quot;. So we have the theme of raw power in here. Now, the interesting thing is that the KoSA are an American organization, yet the USA is known to not be very Catholic- but still, I am not going to claim to understand American Christianity. At any case, we can see that whatever their incarnation, militaristic themes are part of the conspiracy. More than that, we can take into account the fact that the KoSA are called after, well, a Saint- that is a soul of a divine person who connected humanity to the divine. That goes well with Jabreel, as this angel was known for contacting humans in the name of God. That gives us yet another theme to include- the connection between humanity and divinity, with the KoSA serving as the bridge between mortals and angels, between the mundane and the divine.<br /> <br /> So if we are to take all of those factors together, we can start to get a glimpse of what is actually going on with the KoSA- a certain organization revolving a certain archangel with the power to gift humans with divine gifts through stigmata. That angel seems to have formed numerous conspiracies throughout the years- how many we don't know, but wherever there is religion and there is God there are His Hounds seeking out demons. Those hunters, as it seems, serve as a &quot;bridge&quot; between humanity and God- which could explain why the angel didn't Fell through the years. Instead of humans making angels more humanlike, this angel makes human more angel-like, subverting the more expected consequence (as it may be the deal with VASCU). It could very well be that hunters who serve the conspiracy for long enough time may even &quot;transcend&quot; into an angelic status, being covered with Ink and divine power. And if that's the case, we may start getting a glimpse for what is the actual goal of the conspiracy beyond hunting down demons.<br /> <br /> That is, turning humanity into angels.<br /> <br /> Think about it- the goal of the KoSA is, in the end, to stop demons from taking over human lives. God gives without expecting anything in return, humans make sacrifices without expecting anything to return. Their contract is a subversion of the demonic pacts- instead of give and take, it is give and give from both sides. Something weird and strange and based only about faith. Now, if your goal is stopping demons from hiding among humans, the best way to do so it by stopping demons from being able to trade in the souls and life pieces of humans- which could be done only be having another force priority over those aspects. Such as the God Machine. If all humans will become more angelic than human, if their souls and memories and identity are to be sanctioned to God himself through ink and oath, demons would not be able to steal those things for Cover. They'll have no one to trade with, no where to hide, and as such, they would be quickly exposed by the Hounds of God and be brought back to the Almighty's embrace. This is a world where demons can't take anything from you for you are in God's Grace, and as such you are safe and found ion God's care. And when you think about it- this mechanism may very well actually be the goal of the God Machine in relation for humanity in the first place. Stop angels from Falling not by fixing the bug which allows them to fall (that's impossible because entropy) but by making the environment they hide among so hostile that it would become very easy to root them out. A different solution to the same problem. And by doing so, the God Machine will be able to fulfill the ancient promise it made for humanity ages ago, that they, too, could become like God. That they, too, could return to Eden.<br /> <br /> That what rise must fall- but what's fallen may rise again.<br /> <br /> Let's just hope that God won't become sick beforehand.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","user":"LostLight","date":". 02-25-2022, 06:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-02-12T13:20:54-08:00","id":1474514,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LostLight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/1473595-conspiracy-theory-the-supplementary?p=1474511#post1474511\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><b><i>Knights of St Adrian</i></b></span><br /> <br /> Seemingly one of the most modern and relatively localized conspiracies, the KoSA are basically the mercenaries of God. Composed of skip tracers and bail bondsmen, of truck drivers and bikers, the KoSA are so young that their founder is still around, and their reach and methods seem very, well, American. While they are very, very cool, their culture and aesthetics makes it hard for me to view them operating much with the same methods outside of the USA, as skip tracing as a practice is not something I was even aware of before reading about the KoSA, and I viewed as weird until I actually heard a interview of a bail bondsman on YouTube which explained how it actually works. Now, I don't know how common it is around the world- I only know that where I live it isn't really a thing, and as such it makes the KoSA something very, let's say, &amp;quot;exotic&amp;quot;- nevermind how appropriate they are in the game, especially in the context of Demon.<br /> <br /> When the information about the conspiracy was just released, some people were a bit confused about what role do truck drivers and bikers have in a espionage game. The answer was, of course, subversion- the KoSA are the manifestation of brute force. They don't just track down demons, they are also able to punch them so hard in the face that they get out of Cover, allowing angels to track them down. Now, while you could assume that people would get all &amp;quot;You Can't Do It Hunter&amp;quot; over it like they sometimes do, thankfully this conspiracy was written together with Demon's team, which gave a free pass for every crazy thing that the KoSA can do that violates some parts of Demon. Overall, I think that this conspiracy was very much well received, between being a thematic opposition to demons, serving as the Hounds of God, and overall being just plain cool as stigmatic Hell's Angels who go around and kick ass.<br /> <br /> Again, notice that the KoSA keep the theme of monstrous power turned into Endowment- while Stigmatic are generally not being viewed as monsters (having Supernatural Merits instead of Dread Powers), gaining Stigmata could very well count as &amp;quot;power from a monstrous source&amp;quot;- something which the KoSA take upon themselves and utilize in a very distinct way to hunt down demons. Now, something that should be noted is that Stigmata is something which is generally viewed as &amp;quot;accident&amp;quot;- someone stumbles upon a Infrastructure, gets scarred by it and develop strange powers as a result. The more reliable way that hunters of the KoSA gain those abilities makes me wonder if perhaps a more intrinsic system is at play- something which is very God Machine appropriate, as it acts as systems within systems. Something similar to adoption which makes the KoSA &amp;quot;demon blooded&amp;quot; of some kind? Perhaps, but whatever the case may be this conspiracy indeed plays well with the continued theme of &amp;quot;monsters into people through the Vigil&amp;quot; which many conspiracies seem to share.<br /> <br /> Another thing to note is that while I don't know if it was intended, the KoSA very much reminds me of the movie Frailty, where <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"> you have a family chosen by a angel to kill out &amp;quot;demons in human skin&amp;quot;, with those people being able to see through the &amp;quot;human guise&amp;quot; by touch (with demons in that context being just horrible human beings). Not the same thing as the KoSA exactly, but still have enough in common for me to assume it served as an inspiration </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Anyway, overall it seems like there isn't much to chew on in relation to KoSA- we know they were founded by Casey Howards during the 90's, making the conspiracy around 40 years old at best. We know that they have the God Machine as their client, sending them to hunt demons through their contact with their angelic &amp;quot;client&amp;quot;. Really, while the conspiracy is very clearly used by the God Machine, it is hard to say that the KoSA are manipulated by it- they know what they are up to and what they are doing. For them, it's a combination of it being a business and how demons take away parts of people lives in exchange for &amp;quot;benefits&amp;quot;, while God simply rewards them for their loyalty and use. There is not mystery, nothing is hidden- the contract between God and His Hounds is very clear and well understood. Nothing weird, nothing out of the ordinary. Just common people killing demons, move along, move along.<br /> <br /> At least, that's what you could think from the writeup alone.<br /> <br /> Here is the thing- the fact that the KoSA exist makes sense. God needs his soldiers, after all. However, the fact that they are so young is... weird. Sure, with the support of God 30-40 years is nothing in order to make a certain group into a global power- but why did the God Machine waited for so long to create such a organization? I mean, if It can bless humans with demon hunting powers, why it doesn't, like, grant them to any cult in the world? Or at least, any cult which shows loyalty to it? Many, many organizations would have shown great interest in having a regular connection to the God Machine which would feed them info about the location of demons and give them the powers to hunt them down. Why the KoSA and not anyone else? Why to take so long? Why to focus its attention on the US and ignore the rest of the world?<br /> <br /> Thankfully, those questions get answered, at least partially, in Dark Eras 2.<br /> <br /> In Empire of Dust and Gold we get a sidebar which details a organization which is uncannily similar to the modern KoSA- the Soldiers of Jabreel. Again, we have a organization of militaristic hunters who serve as &amp;quot;holy hunters&amp;quot; chosen by God to fight demons, including special abilities bestowed upon them which allow those hunters to fight their enemies heads on. Now. while the writeup does not outright connects them to the modern conspiracy, the writer have confirmed that the Soldiers were a different incarnation of the Knights, one which was more appropriate for the spirit of the Era and which fit better the local area.<br /> <br /> One which may be still fitting in modern times. <br /> <br /> Here is the thing- technically we are not aware of the SoJ getting disbanded at any point. Like, we can assume it happened (especially after, you know, colonialism), but we don't have an actual date for 